  },
  "presentation_summary": [
    "A man in his late 60s was found unresponsive at home after suspected ingestion of 100 tablets of 75-mg metoprolol tartrate, total 7500 mg, with possible lorazepam and hydroxyzine coingestion.",
    "The estimated ingestion-to-hospital interval was 6 to 8 hours; initial ED evaluation showed GCS 7, shallow breathing, BP 63/57 mmHg, HR 73/min, temperature 35.6 C, RR 10/min, and SpO2 70%.",
    "He was intubated, received crystalloid and norepinephrine, and initially improved, but later developed bradycardia around 44/min, MAP near 60 mmHg, and echocardiographic EF below 20%.",
    "Glucagon, calcium chloride, escalating norepinephrine/epinephrine/vasopressin, hyperinsulinemic euglycemia therapy, intravenous lipid emulsion, bicarbonate, and CRRT were used before ECMO.",
    "Despite HIE and lipid therapy he deteriorated to HR 26/min with complete heart block, temporary pacing, worsening acidosis, pulmonary edema, and refractory shock requiring VA-ECMO.",
    "ECMO response, later weaning, and recovery are retained as non-ranking treatment/outcome evidence."
  ],
